/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State and prove Varignon's Theorem of Moments for a system of coplanar concurrent forces.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Statement and Physical Significance:) Tj
T*
(   Varignon's Theorem \(Principle of Moments\) states that the algebraic sum of the) Tj
T*
(   moments of a system of coplanar forces about any arbitrary point in their plane) Tj
T*
(   is identically equal to the moment of their resultant force about the same point.) Tj
T*
(   In civil engineering, this principle forms the foundational basis for determining) Tj
T*
(   the spatial line of action of resultant wind, seismic, and hydrostatic force systems.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Vector Proof:) Tj
T*
(   Consider a system of 'n' concurrent forces F1, F2, F3, ..., Fn acting at a common point P.) Tj
T*
(   Let 'O' be any reference origin in the plane, and let vector 'r' denote the position) Tj
T*
(   vector of the concurrent point of concurrency P with respect to origin O \(r = OP\).) Tj
T*
(   By vector addition, the resultant force vector R is given by:) Tj
T*
(   R = F1 + F2 + F3 + ... + Fn = Sigma F_i) Tj
T*
(   Taking the cross product of the position vector 'r' with the resultant vector R:) Tj
T*
(   M_O\(R\) = r x R = r x \(F1 + F2 + F3 + ... + Fn\)) Tj
T*
(   Using the distributive property of vector cross products over vector addition:) Tj
T*
(   r x \(Sigma F_i\) = \(r x F1\) + \(r x F2\) + \(r x F3\) + ... + \(r x Fn\)) Tj
T*
(   By definition, the term \(r x F_i\) represents the moment vector M_O\(F_i\) of the individual) Tj
T*
(   constituent force F_i about reference point O.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, we obtain the exact scalar and vector equivalence:) Tj
T*
(   M_O\(R\) = Sigma M_O\(F_i\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Scalar Resolution and Engineering Application:) Tj
T*
(   Let point P have Cartesian coordinates \(x, y\) relative to origin O. Resolving forces:) Tj
T*
(   R_x = Sigma F_ix, R_y = Sigma F_iy) Tj
T*
(   The moment of the resultant about the origin is:) Tj
T*
(   M_O = \(x * R_y\) - \(y * R_x\)) Tj
T*
(   Each force component contributes: M_Oi = \(x * F_iy\) - \(y * F_ix\).) Tj
T*
(   Summing all components: Sigma M_Oi = x * \(Sigma F_iy\) - y * \(Sigma F_ix\) = x*R_y - y*R_x = M_O.) Tj
T*
(   Thus, the theorem is proved rigorously in both vector and scalar formulations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the physical significance of a Free Body Diagram \(FBD\) and detail the systematic steps to construct it for civil engineering structures.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition and Physical Significance:) Tj
T*
(   A Free Body Diagram \(FBD\) is an analytical graphical representation of a structural body) Tj
T*
(   or subsystem completely isolated \(freed\) from its surrounding physical environment.) Tj
T*
(   It exposes all external active loads \(gravity weight, applied surface pressures, wind\)) Tj
T*
(   and reactive restraint forces/couples exerted on the isolated body by adjacent supports) Tj
T*
(   and connections. Constructing an accurate FBD is the critical first step in static analysis;) Tj
T*
(   omitting forces or misrepresenting kinematic constraints causes erroneous reaction) Tj
T*
(   calculations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Classification of Forces Acting on a Free Body:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Active Forces: Known externally applied loads such as vehicular traffic loads, point loads,) Tj
T*
(   hydrostatic pressures, and self-weight \(W = m*g\) acting through the center of gravity.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
