(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State Lami's Theorem and provide its complete rigorous proof using the Sine Rule of triangles.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Statement of Lami's Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   If three coplanar concurrent forces acting at a particle are in static equilibrium, each) Tj
T*
(   force is directly proportional to the sine of the angle included between the other two forces.) Tj
T*
(   Let forces P, Q, and R be in equilibrium, and let alpha, beta, and gamma denote the angles) Tj
T*
(   opposite to P, Q, and R respectively \(alpha is angle between Q and R, beta between P and R,) Tj
T*
(   gamma between P and Q\). Then:) Tj
T*
(   P / sin\(alpha\) = Q / sin\(beta\) = R / sin\(gamma\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Rigorous Proof via Triangle Law of Forces:) Tj
T*
(   Since forces P, Q, and R are in static equilibrium, their vector sum is zero: P + Q + R = 0.) Tj
T*
(   By the Triangle Law of Forces, these three vectors can be arranged head-to-tail to form) Tj
T*
(   a closed vector triangle ABC, where vector AB = P, vector BC = Q, and vector CA = R.) Tj
T*
(   Let us determine the interior angles of triangle ABC in terms of external angles alpha, beta,) Tj
T*
(   gamma:) Tj
T*
(   - Vector P is along AB and vector Q is along BC. The angle between directions of P and Q is) Tj
T*
(   gamma.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, exterior angle at vertex B is gamma => Interior angle B = 180 deg - gamma.) Tj
T*
(   - Vector Q is along BC and vector R is along CA. The angle between directions of Q and R is) Tj
T*
(   alpha.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, exterior angle at vertex C is alpha => Interior angle C = 180 deg - alpha.) Tj
T*
(   - Vector R is along CA and vector P is along AB. The angle between directions of R and P is) Tj
T*
(   beta.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, exterior angle at vertex A is beta => Interior angle A = 180 deg - beta.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Application of Sine Rule:) Tj
T*
(   Applying the Law of Sines to triangle ABC:) Tj
T*
(   AB / sin\(C\) = BC / sin\(A\) = CA / sin\(B\)) Tj
T*
(   Substituting the vector magnitudes and interior angles:) Tj
T*
(   P / sin\(180 deg - alpha\) = Q / sin\(180 deg - beta\) = R / sin\(180 deg - gamma\)) Tj
T*
(   Since sin\(180 deg - theta\) = sin\(theta\) for any angle theta, we obtain:) Tj
T*
(   P / sin\(alpha\) = Q / sin\(beta\) = R / sin\(gamma\)) Tj
T*
(   This completes the mathematical proof of Lami's Theorem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the concept of a Force Couple, prove that its moment is a free vector invariant of the reference origin, and explain its engineering applications.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition and Physical Characteristics of a Couple:) Tj
T*
(   A force couple consists of two parallel forces that possess identical magnitudes F,) Tj
T*
(   opposite directions, and are separated by a non-zero perpendicular distance d.) Tj
T*
(   Resultant Linear Force: R = F + \(-F\) = 0 N. Thus, a pure couple produces no linear) Tj
T*
(   translation whatsoever; its sole physical effect on a rigid body is pure rotation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Proof of Origin Invariance \(Free Vector\):) Tj
T*
ET

