(   [Problem Statement]: A system of three parallel vertical forces acts on a horizontal beam: 30 kN down at x = 1 m, 50 kN down at x = 3 m, and 20 kN down at x = 6 m. Determine the magnitude, direction, and exact position of the single resultant force.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Resultant Force Magnitude and Direction:) Tj
T*
(   All forces act parallel to the vertical y-axis.) Tj
T*
(   Summing vertical components \(taking downward as negative\):) Tj
T*
(   Sigma Fy = - 30 - 50 - 20 = - 100 kN.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, the single resultant force has magnitude R = 100 kN directed vertically downward.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Application of Varignon's Theorem for Resultant Location:) Tj
T*
(   Taking the origin at x = 0 \(left end of beam\) and summing moments about x = 0:) Tj
T*
(   Clockwise moments about origin:) Tj
T*
(   Sigma M_O = \(30 kN * 1.0 m\) + \(50 kN * 3.0 m\) + \(20 kN * 6.0 m\)) Tj
T*
(   Sigma M_O = 30 + 150 + 120 = 300 kN-m \(clockwise\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Location x_bar of Resultant Line of Action:) Tj
T*
(   According to Varignon's Theorem, the moment of the resultant force about the origin) Tj
T*
(   must equal the algebraic sum of the moments of the individual forces:) Tj
T*
(   R * x_bar = Sigma M_O) Tj
T*
(   100 kN * x_bar = 300 kN-m) Tj
T*
(   x_bar = 300 / 100 = 3.00 m from the left origin x = 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   The three parallel forces can be replaced completely by a single concentrated vertical) Tj
T*
(   downward force of 100 kN acting at a distance of 3.00 m from the left end.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
