BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the reduction of a system of coplanar non-concurrent forces into a single equivalent force and a couple at a chosen base point, and describe when this system reduces to a single resultant force.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Concept of Force-Couple Reduction:) Tj
T*
(   Any general system of coplanar non-concurrent forces F1, F2, ..., Fn acting at points) Tj
T*
(   with position vectors r1, r2, ..., rn can be transformed into an equipollent system) Tj
T*
(   at an arbitrary point O consisting of:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) A single resultant force vector R acting through point O.) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) A resultant couple moment vector M_O.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Step-by-Step Analytical Formulation:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Compute the resultant force vector by summing all force vectors:) Tj
T*
(   R = Sigma F_i = \(Sigma F_ix\) i + \(Sigma F_iy\) j) Tj
T*
(   Magnitude: |R| = sqrt\( \(Sigma F_ix\)^2 + \(Sigma F_iy\)^2 \)) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Transfer each force F_i to base point O. By the principle of transmissibility) Tj
T*
(   and force couples, moving a force F_i from point r_i to point O requires adding) Tj
T*
(   a couple equal to its moment about O: M_Oi = r_i x F_i.) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Sum all moments and external couples to obtain the total couple at O:) Tj
T*
(   M_O = Sigma \(r_i x F_i\) + Sigma C_j) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Conditions for Further Simplification to a Single Resultant Force:) Tj
T*
(   Case A [Single Resultant Force]: If resultant force R != 0, the force-couple system \(R, M_O\)) Tj
T*
(   can be further reduced to a single resultant force R alone, located at a perpendicular) Tj
T*
ET

