(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A simply supported beam AB of span 7 m carries a uniformly varying load of zero at left support A increasing linearly to 14 kN/m at midspan \(x = 3.5 m\), and then decreasing linearly back to zero at right support B \(x = 7 m\). Determine support reactions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Loading Symmetry and Total Load Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   The loading is an isosceles triangle over the entire 7.0 m span:) Tj
T*
(   - Base b = 7.0 m; Peak intensity at apex w_max = 14.0 kN/m at x = 3.5 m.) Tj
T*
(   Total load W_total equals the area of the triangular loading diagram:) Tj
T*
(   W_total = \(1/2\) * base * peak_height = \(1/2\) * 7.0 m * 14.0 kN/m = 49.0 kN.) Tj
T*
(   Centroid of an isosceles triangle lies symmetrically at its midspan:) Tj
T*
(   x_bar = 7.0 / 2 = 3.50 m from support A.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Equilibrium Equations:) Tj
T*
(   Because the beam and loading are perfectly symmetric about midspan:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Moment Equilibrium about A \(Sigma M_A = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   \(R_B * 7.0 m\) - \(W_total * 3.50 m\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   7.0 * R_B = 49.0 * 3.50 = 171.5 kN-m) Tj
T*
(   R_B = 171.5 / 7.0 = 24.50 kN \(upward\).) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Vertical Force Equilibrium \(Sigma Fy = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   R_A + R_B - W_total = 0) Tj
T*
(   R_A + 24.50 = 49.0) Tj
T*
(   R_A = 49.0 - 24.50 = 24.50 kN \(upward\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   Due to loading symmetry, the support reactions are identical: R_A = 24.50 kN, R_B = 24.50 kN.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 28:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Determine the tension in each of the three cables AB, BC, and CD that support two vertical loads of 10 kN at joint B and 15 kN at joint C. Joints B and C settle such that cable AB is at 30 deg, cable CD is at 45 deg, and cable BC is horizontal.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. System Geometry and FBD of Joints:) Tj
T*
(   - Joint B connects cable AB \(inclined at 30 deg above horizontal\), horizontal cable BC, and) Tj
T*
(   vertical load 10 kN.) Tj
T*
(   - Joint C connects horizontal cable BC, cable CD \(inclined at 45 deg above horizontal\), and) Tj
T*
(   vertical load 15 kN.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Equilibrium of Joint B:) Tj
T*
(   Let T_AB be tension in cable AB, and T_BC be tension in horizontal cable BC.) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Vertical Equilibrium \(Sigma Fy = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   T_AB * sin\(30 deg\) - 10 kN = 0) Tj
T*
(   T_AB * 0.500 = 10 kN => T_AB = 20.00 kN.) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Horizontal Equilibrium \(Sigma Fx = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   T_BC - T_AB * cos\(30 deg\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   T_BC = 20.00 * cos\(30 deg\) = 20.00 * 0.8660 = 17.32 kN.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Equilibrium of Joint C:) Tj
T*
(   Let T_CD be tension in cable CD.) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Vertical Equilibrium \(Sigma Fy = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   T_CD * sin\(45 deg\) - 15 kN = 0) Tj
T*
(   T_CD * 0.7071 = 15 kN => T_CD = 15 / 0.7071 = 21.21 kN.) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Horizontal Equilibrium Check \(Sigma Fx = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   T_CD * cos\(45 deg\) - T_BC = 0) Tj
T*
(   21.21 * 0.7071 = 15.00 kN != 17.32 kN!) Tj
T*
(   Note: For exact compatibility, cable BC must slope slightly or cable CD angle adjusts to) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
