12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A propped cantilever beam AB of span L = 6 m is fixed at A and supported on a simple roller prop at B. Explain why this beam is statically indeterminate to the first degree and outline the classical method of consistent deformations to solve for the prop reaction.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Degree of Static Indeterminacy Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   - Fixed support at A provides 3 reaction components: vertical shear R_A, horizontal force H_A,) Tj
T*
(   and fixed restraining moment M_A.) Tj
T*
(   - Roller prop at B provides 1 vertical reaction component: R_B.) Tj
T*
(   Total unknown support reactions: r = 3 + 1 = 4.) Tj
T*
(   Total equations of static equilibrium for coplanar forces: n = 3 \(Sigma Fx = 0, Sigma Fy = 0,) Tj
T*
(   Sigma M = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   Degree of Static Indeterminacy: DSI = r - n = 4 - 3 = 1 \(Indeterminate of 1st degree\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Method of Consistent Deformations \(Force Method\):) Tj
T*
(   Step 1 [Primary Determinate Structure]: Select the prop reaction R_B as the redundant force.) Tj
T*
(   Remove the roller at B to obtain a primary cantilever beam subjected to applied loads alone.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2 [Deflection due to Applied Loads]: Calculate the downward vertical deflection delta_B0) Tj
T*
(   at free end B under the applied external load system \(e.g. For UDL w: delta_B0 = w*L^4 /) Tj
T*
(   \(8*E*I\)\).) Tj
T*
(   Step 3 [Flexibility Coefficient]: Apply a unit upward dummy load \(1 kN\) at point B on the) Tj
T*
(   cantilever) Tj
T*
(   and calculate the upward deflection f_BB \(flexibility coefficient: f_BB = L^3 / \(3*E*I\)\).) Tj
T*
(   Step 4 [Geometric Compatibility Equation]: At the actual roller support, net vertical) Tj
T*
(   deflection is zero:) Tj
T*
(   delta_B = delta_B0 - \(R_B * f_BB\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   R_B = delta_B0 / f_BB = [ \(w*L^4 / 8*E*I\) ] / [ \(L^3 / 3*E*I\) ] = \(3/8\) * w * L.) Tj
T*
(   Step 5 [Equilibrium Reactions]: With R_B determined, R_A = \(5/8\)*w*L and M_A = \(1/8\)*w*L^2) Tj
T*
(   follow from statics.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 30:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Summarize the comprehensive workflow for analyzing equilibrium in complex civil engineering structures, highlighting best practices and common pitfalls.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Best Practices in Engineering Mechanics Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Systematic Free Body Diagrams: Always isolate the structural component, replacing all) Tj
T*
(   physical) Tj
T*
(   supports with correct kinematic restraint reactions before writing any equilibrium equations.) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Coordinate Consistency: Define explicit Cartesian coordinate axes \(+x right, +y up\) and a) Tj
T*
(   fixed) Tj
T*
(   rotational sense \(+CCW\). Adhere strictly to this sign convention throughout calculations.) Tj
T*
(   \(c\) Strategic Choice of Moment Origin: When taking moment equilibrium \(Sigma M = 0\), always) Tj
T*
(   select) Tj
T*
(   the point through which the greatest number of unknown forces pass \(e.g., a pinned support\).) Tj
T*
(   This decouples the equations, yielding a single equation with one unknown.) Tj
T*
ET

